WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.233 --> 00:00:01.568
Congrats to you tonight.

00:00:01.568 --> 00:00:03.103
I just want to note that once again,

00:00:03.103 --> 00:00:04.871
Donald Trump cost my party Senate seats.

00:00:04.871 --> 00:00:06.473
I was advising these same Georgia

00:00:06.473 --> 00:00:07.941
runoffs in 2020

00:00:07.941 --> 00:00:09.142
when he lost those seats

00:00:09.142 --> 00:00:11.011
by touting the election lies

00:00:11.011 --> 00:00:12.712
when it was Perdue and Kelly Loeffler.

00:00:12.712 --> 00:00:13.413
Once again,

00:00:13.413 --> 00:00:14.614
he put his hand on the scale

00:00:14.614 --> 00:00:15.482
and put up a candidate

00:00:15.482 --> 00:00:16.916
who's highly unqualified

00:00:16.916 --> 00:00:18.852
Herschel Walker to be in the U.S. Senate.

00:00:18.852 --> 00:00:20.253
And so now we are in a position

00:00:20.253 --> 00:00:22.389
where we have to come around and realize

00:00:22.389 --> 00:00:24.391
we are not even merely losing under him.

00:00:24.591 --> 00:00:26.793
The fact that in Biden's economy,

00:00:26.793 --> 00:00:28.495
how poorly the country is doing,

00:00:28.495 --> 00:00:30.463
the fact that by and large voters, right,

00:00:30.463 --> 00:00:30.997
left or center

00:00:30.997 --> 00:00:33.133
say we're on the wrong trajectory,

00:00:33.133 --> 00:00:34.834
yet they're not voting for change.

00:00:34.834 --> 00:00:36.269
That since 1934

00:00:36.269 --> 00:00:36.903
that they have

00:00:36.903 --> 00:00:38.004
not actually

00:00:38.004 --> 00:00:39.172
had an incumbent

00:00:39.172 --> 00:00:40.707
not lose seats in the Senate.

00:00:40.707 --> 00:00:41.641
That is an indictment

00:00:41.641 --> 00:00:42.609
of the Republican Party

00:00:42.609 --> 00:00:44.944
we need to wake up because we are losing.

00:00:44.944 --> 00:00:46.379
Can I just say something about voters

00:00:46.379 --> 00:00:48.348
here in this whole midterm election

00:00:48.348 --> 00:00:50.583
we saw a lot of split tickets.

00:00:51.217 --> 00:00:53.086
People were voting for candidates.

00:00:53.086 --> 00:00:54.954
They were not just voting up

00:00:54.954 --> 00:00:55.789
and down the line.

00:00:55.789 --> 00:00:56.589
They were taking a look

00:00:56.589 --> 00:01:00.193
at a candidate's character and experience

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:02.228
and saying, you know, I want this person,

00:01:02.228 --> 00:01:03.329
you know, I'm going to vote

00:01:03.329 --> 00:01:05.265
for the Republican governor

00:01:05.265 --> 00:01:06.032
in New Hampshire.

00:01:06.032 --> 00:01:06.666
That's great.

00:01:06.666 --> 00:01:07.834
Yeah, it's a

00:01:07.834 --> 00:01:09.302
it's a it's a development

00:01:09.302 --> 00:01:09.936
in this country.

00:01:09.936 --> 00:01:10.470
You know,

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:11.404
there are a couple of states

00:01:11.404 --> 00:01:12.839
that are famous for it, Montana

00:01:12.839 --> 00:01:13.773
being one of them.

00:01:13.773 --> 00:01:14.841
But it doesn't happen

00:01:14.841 --> 00:01:16.142
all the time in this country.

00:01:16.142 --> 00:01:17.310
And now

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:18.778
I think this is a great development.

00:01:18.778 --> 00:01:20.013
People are paying attention

00:01:20.013 --> 00:01:21.714
because the stakes are so high,

00:01:21.714 --> 00:01:22.949
especially if we keep talking

00:01:22.949 --> 00:01:24.451
about candidate quality.

00:01:24.451 --> 00:01:26.219
And we only talk about it

00:01:26.219 --> 00:01:27.353
on the Republican side

00:01:27.353 --> 00:01:28.288
saying they have negative

00:01:29.589 --> 00:01:30.957
but I just want to say,

00:01:30.957 --> 00:01:31.724
you know better,

00:01:31.724 --> 00:01:33.760
why not is an extraordinary human being.

00:01:35.028 --> 00:01:37.230
There's a reason he's recognizable

00:01:37.664 --> 00:01:38.498
in our community.

00:01:38.498 --> 00:01:40.400
As somebody who was working

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:42.535
hard for people when he wasn't famous,

00:01:42.535 --> 00:01:44.170
working hard for people when he was name,

00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:46.473
was always believing in these causes

00:01:46.473 --> 00:01:47.974
when they were not popular

00:01:47.974 --> 00:01:50.677
and so he's somebody who has a resonance

00:01:50.677 --> 00:01:51.544
that he has with people.

00:01:51.544 --> 00:01:53.012
People believe him.

00:01:53.012 --> 00:01:53.880
And and I just

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:55.648
I don't want to only be

00:01:55.648 --> 00:01:57.784
you had a bad guy on the Republican side.

00:01:57.784 --> 00:01:58.918
You had a good man

00:01:58.918 --> 00:02:00.186
on the Democratic side nationally.

00:02:00.186 --> 00:02:01.421
And he didn't change.

00:02:01.421 --> 00:02:04.057
He didn't he could have

00:02:04.324 --> 00:02:06.192
dragged Herschel Walker

00:02:06.192 --> 00:02:07.360
through the end zone,

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:08.495
through the whatever.

00:02:08.495 --> 00:02:09.696
What I was looking

00:02:10.897 --> 00:02:11.531
what you got,

00:02:11.531 --> 00:02:13.066
but he could have dragged him.

00:02:13.066 --> 00:02:15.101
There was so much material,

00:02:15.101 --> 00:02:17.570
but he was a man of character and said,

00:02:18.238 --> 00:02:19.405
I know who I am

00:02:19.405 --> 00:02:20.507
and I'm going to run the race.

00:02:20.507 --> 00:02:21.641
Now. I did.

00:02:21.674 --> 00:02:23.309
I'm I like my negative ads.

00:02:23.309 --> 00:02:23.743
They did.

00:02:23.743 --> 00:02:24.677
They did run their fair

00:02:24.677 --> 00:02:26.079
share of negative ads.

00:02:26.079 --> 00:02:27.780
50, 60, $70 million

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:28.815
in character smashing.

00:02:28.815 --> 00:02:29.916
And so it wasn't

00:02:29.916 --> 00:02:31.151
it wasn't a clean campaign.

00:02:31.151 --> 00:02:32.719
They could have been a lot.

00:02:32.719 --> 00:02:33.820
They did what they had to do.

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:36.289
But I think I think on Van's point,

00:02:36.289 --> 00:02:37.257
one thing I learned about this

00:02:37.257 --> 00:02:38.491
entire election,

00:02:38.491 --> 00:02:41.427
people with personal brands apart

00:02:41.427 --> 00:02:43.496
from somebody else,

00:02:43.496 --> 00:02:45.331
did well in both parties.

00:02:45.331 --> 00:02:47.634
Warnock had a brand apart from politics,

00:02:48.201 --> 00:02:50.136
but the camps and the DeWine's

00:02:50.136 --> 00:02:51.004
and the DeSantis

00:02:51.004 --> 00:02:53.406
and the Abbotts and the Republican Party,

00:02:53.406 --> 00:02:55.842
they didn't depend on Donald Trump

00:02:55.842 --> 00:02:57.677
or some other person

00:02:57.677 --> 00:02:59.112
to define who they are.

00:02:59.112 --> 00:03:01.114
And I do think this ticket splitting,

00:03:01.681 --> 00:03:02.615
Gloria,

00:03:02.615 --> 00:03:04.317
there's something about being able

00:03:04.317 --> 00:03:06.619
to define and own who you are

00:03:06.619 --> 00:03:08.454
that people are responding to.

00:03:08.454 --> 00:03:09.422
They don't want you to be

00:03:09.422 --> 00:03:11.124
a wholly owned subsidiary

00:03:11.124 --> 00:03:12.926
of some other person or thing.

00:03:12.926 --> 00:03:15.361
They just want you to be who you are

00:03:15.361 --> 00:03:16.462
and we're not.

00:03:16.462 --> 00:03:18.331
And you got that right in this race.

00:03:18.331 --> 00:03:20.433
Walker got tagged with with Trump

00:03:20.433 --> 00:03:21.901
and the candidates in these Senate races

00:03:21.901 --> 00:03:23.303
that got tagged that way with Trump

00:03:23.303 --> 00:03:25.371
all across the board lost.

00:03:25.838 --> 00:03:27.207
And that's a big lesson for the next.

00:03:27.207 --> 00:03:29.108
Like every Republican in this country

00:03:29.108 --> 00:03:30.009
ought to hold Donald Trump

00:03:30.009 --> 00:03:30.810
accountable for this.

00:03:30.810 --> 00:03:31.277
And I think

00:03:31.277 --> 00:03:32.178
the part that bothers me

00:03:32.178 --> 00:03:32.645
the most

00:03:32.645 --> 00:03:33.646
and I spent a lot of time

00:03:33.646 --> 00:03:35.281
trying to dissect this,

00:03:35.281 --> 00:03:36.082
is that Donald Trump,

00:03:36.082 --> 00:03:37.083
it's not out of ignorance

00:03:37.083 --> 00:03:38.685
or miscalculation.

00:03:38.685 --> 00:03:39.919
It's out of selfishness.

00:03:39.919 --> 00:03:41.287
He's not picking these candidates

00:03:41.287 --> 00:03:42.088
because he thinks it's

00:03:42.088 --> 00:03:43.289
